## Prepare an isolated snapshot

Read [scripts/prepare_review.py](scripts/prepare_review.py) for its CLI, then run
it once per review scope after push success and capability checks. It requires
Python 3.11+ and Git 2.46+ (to disable lazy object fetching):

```text
python3 <skill-directory>/scripts/prepare_review.py --repo <source-repository> --snapshot-parent <permitted-parent> --base <full-base-sha> --head <full-pushed-sha>
```

For a new ref with no target baseline, replace `--base <sha>` with `--root`. Never
use root mode to hide a missing old SHA for an existing ref.

The script creates a standalone temporary Git repository at the pinned head,
retains the base for comparison, and prints JSON with `directory`, `base_sha`,
`head_sha`, and `baseline_kind` (`commit` or `empty-tree`). Root mode creates the
empty tree in the snapshot's object format. The helper accepts a repository root,
subdirectory, linked worktree, or bare repository. It preserves only existing
system/global `safe.directory` entries for source discovery, then trusts the
validated Git directory for local object access. Its Git commands exclude other
inherited settings and disable hooks and external attributes; content filters
do not run during checkout. Interrupted setup attempts cleanup; if cleanup fails,
the helper reports the remaining path without hiding the setup error. It neither
pushes nor launches a model. Add remote/ref
identity and baseline provenance to the task message; its output alone is not
proof of a successful push. Missing commits or setup failure mean `incomplete`.
All reachable Git objects for the pinned commits must already exist locally.
Partial clones with missing objects fail before creating a snapshot; the helper
does not hydrate them from a remote. Materializing objects is separate authorized
work, not an automatic review fallback.

Keep the original working tree untouched, even if it is dirty. Retain the
snapshot until the reviewer stops. Then remove only the temporary directory
created by the script; never remove a user workspace or clean an active review.
If task startup or access verification fails, remove the unused snapshot and
report `incomplete`.
Do not fetch submodules or Git LFS content implicitly. If missing content prevents
reviewing affected behavior, state the gap and return `incomplete`.

## Start a fresh background reviewer

Read [references/reviewer.md](references/reviewer.md). Give the reviewer the path
to that reference, the snapshot directory, push identity, pinned SHAs, original
requirements and constraints, and relevant repository rules/documents. Preserve
the user's requirements without adding the author's interpretation. The reviewer
must read project instructions from the pinned snapshot.

Do not pass the parent transcript, implementation plan, reasoning, self-review,
prior conclusions, or claims that tests passed. Do not reuse an agent or memory
from implementation or a previous review. Let the reviewer collect test evidence.
Do not substitute a fork of the author conversation for a fresh context.

Verify access to the created snapshot. If denied, return `incomplete`; never fall
back to the author's checkout. Use the session's model by default. Do not reuse
an implementation agent or spawn additional reviewers.

Track each task with its remote/ref, base, head, and snapshot path. Announce that
review is running, then continue the user's work without waiting for it. Review
scope is fixed; a later push starts a separate task. Do not recursively review
reviewer activity. This version is session-scoped: it does not guarantee execution
or notification after the session closes.

## Report completion

Only report a completed review after receiving its actual result. In the current
conversation, name the remote/ref, reviewed SHAs, baseline provenance, and status:

- `completed`: the defined scope was inspected, with findings or none found.
- `incomplete`: setup, capability, execution, cancellation, or coverage failed.

Include the reviewer's evidence, checks actually run, and material limitations.
Keep confirmed defects separate from unresolved questions. No findings means no
supported defects were found, not that the code is guaranteed correct. A partial
result or missing requirement needed to judge behavior cannot be called complete.

If a newer push has occurred, label the result as applying to the older SHA. Do
not automatically fix, revert, commit, or push based on findings. If the user asks
for a fix, handle it as a separate authorized change.
